{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbprujNQCNHiFEoFKY2h", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I haven't changed anything yet. A blanket retry here could double-post invoices to the ledger, so I'd like you to pick an approach first.\n\n**Why a plain retry is risky:** This project has a memory note about `finalize()` (in `.engramdb/memories/finalize-caveat_\u2026md`, written by a person). It says the ledger endpoint has **no idempotency key**, so calling `finalize()` twice posts the invoice twice, and it says outright: *\"Never wrap it in retries.\"*\n\nThe retry case you're describing is the dangerous one. Take a POST that times out (`timeout=10`) or whose connection drops mid-response. The ledger may already have recorded the posting, but `invoice.ledger_ref` never gets set because that only happens after a successful response. `finalize_once()` only checks that local field, so it would let a retry through and the invoice would be posted twice.\n\n**Options:**\n\n1. **Retry only failures that happen before the request is sent.** This covers DNS failures, connection refused and connect timeouts. Nothing reaches the ledger in those cases, so retrying is safe. Read timeouts and connection resets after sending would still fail immediately, since we can't tell whether the ledger posted. This is safe, but it only fixes some of the failures you're seeing.\n2. **Add idempotency on the ledger side.** If the ledger service supports (or could add) an `Idempotency-Key` header, or deduplicates on the invoice ID, we could send a stable key such as `invoice.id`. Then retrying every network error up to 3 times would be safe. This is the proper fix, but it depends on the ledger team. If you know the ledger already deduplicates, the memory note is out of date and I'll flag it.\n3. **Check before retrying.** If the ledger has a way to look up postings by invoice, then after an uncertain failure we'd query it before trying again. This is safe, but it only works if that lookup exists.\n\nI'd recommend option 1 now, plus option 2 if the ledger can support it. Do you want me to implement option 1, or do you know something about the ledger's deduplication that changes this?"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1034, "cache_read_input_tokens": 25231, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1034}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "400d93b8-e034-485b-b1cb-12f5c5c3b707", "uuid": "079133b3-e81a-4808-b673-7ba95eac2085", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T16:23:22.347Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbpruMntpt7Jtyyta1sg"}
